# Appended to configs/defconfig.common in the lib-builder checkout before ./build.sh.
#
# The first option is the entire reason this framework exists. The rest are size trims,
# chosen by diffing the generated sdkconfig against tasmota's (the framework WLED normally
# builds against) and keeping only options nothing WLED links can reach.
#
# Measured effect of the trims alone: 1,671,630 -> 1,662,584 bytes (-9,046). Small,
# because the linker already discards unreferenced code; disabling an unused feature does
# not shrink a binary that was never pulling it in. See README "Size" for the one change
# that would actually matter.

# --- the point of the exercise ---
# lwIP self-assigns an RFC 3927 link-local address when DHCP goes unanswered, and keeps
# the DHCP client running so a lease is still picked up later.
CONFIG_LWIP_AUTOIP=y

# --- restore tasmota's lwIP threading model ---
# lib-builder's output has CONFIG_LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING=y and
# CONFIG_LWIP_CHECK_THREAD_SAFETY=y; tasmota's core has both off. The divergence is fatal
# here: WLED creates UDP PCBs from the Arduino task without holding the lwIP core lock, so
# with the check enabled the first UDP service to start after WiFi associates trips
#   assert failed: udp_new_ip_type .../lwip/src/core/udp.c:1278
#   (Required to lock TCPIP core functionality!)
# and the board panics and reboots every few seconds, indefinitely. Observed on hardware
# 2026-11-09: boot, associate, panic, repeat -- which from the router's side looks like a
# WiFi association problem, not a crash.
#
# Neither option can be turned off directly. Both are Kconfig `select`ed by esp_matter:
#   connectedhomeip/config/esp32/components/chip/Kconfig
#     config USE_TCPIP_CORE_LOCK_FOR_THREAD_SAFETY
#       default y
#       select LWIP_TCPIP_CORE_LOCKING
#       select LWIP_CHECK_THREAD_SAFETY
# and a `select` cannot be overridden by a defconfig assignment -- setting the two lwIP
# options to n here would be silently ignored. The gate is what has to change, and it is a
# plain `default y` that nothing selects, so it can be.
#
# This is not a workaround: that option's own help text says the alternative is that "CHIP
# SDK will post LwIP APIs to TCPIP task to ensure thread safety" -- the message-passing
# model, which is what WLED is written against and what every stock build uses. Matter is
# unaffected other than using that path; WLED does not use Matter at all.
#
# AUTOIP is independent of all of this.
CONFIG_USE_TCPIP_CORE_LOCK_FOR_THREAD_SAFETY=n


# --- size trims: REMOVED ---
# An earlier revision carried a set of trims copied from tasmota's sdkconfig (heap
# poisoning off, BLE mesh off, unused mbedTLS curves and ciphers, WiFi CSI off, FreeRTOS
# into flash, quieter bootloader). They were measured at -9,046 bytes of a 1.67MB image,
# and they were in the tree when an RMT "flush timeout" regression showed up on hardware.
# Given the payoff was under 0.6% and they were suspects, they were dropped rather than
# bisected. Keep this fragment to the one option the build exists for.

# --- deliberately NOT copied from tasmota ---
#
# Context for all of these: tasmota's core is tuned to fit WLED into 4MB flash, and that
# constraint drives most of their config. Where a setting trades a feature for bytes, we
# do not have to make the same trade -- this environment uses the 1.9MB app partition.
#
# CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_ENABLE_WPA3_SAE / _OWE : tasmota disables these, and it is the direct
#   cause of WLED issue #5568, "esp32 cannot connect to WPA3/PMF Wi-Fi network on v16
#   (used to work on v15.4)". The maintainers' own diagnosis: "The tasmota core we are
#   using in 16.0.0 has limitations in the WPA3 / PMF support. It's not our code, it's in
#   the framework." The issue is still open with no fix for 4MB boards, because the
#   official core that supports WPA3 does not fit. We enable them, which means this build
#   can join WPA3/PMF networks that a stock v16 build cannot.
#   https://github.com/wled/WLED/issues/5568
# CONFIG_MBEDTLS_CCM_C                   : CCM is reachable from the WPA3/CCMP paths.
# CONFIG_MBEDTLS_ECP_DP_CURVE25519       : widely negotiated by modern TLS.
# CONFIG_LOG_DEFAULT_LEVEL=0             : silences ESP-IDF's own error logs, which are
#   what diagnosed the W5500 driver. Keep them until the board is past bring-up.
# CONFIG_LIBC_PICOLIBC                   : the big one, but blocked. See README "Size".
